{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["עשן כלו — half of it has qamatz and half of it has pataḥ, for it is a verb form: 'he made it all smoke.' But עָשָׁן, which is a noun, is all qamatz. כְּעֶשֶׁן — like smoke, pumia in Old French. And if it were a noun he would have had to say כַּעֲשַׁן הַכִּבְשָׁן, as one says מִן דָּבָר — [with min; example of] noun with construct: מִן בָּקַר זְבַח הַשְּׁלָמִים. So too one says מִן הַבַּיִת. יִמָּלֵא עָשָׁן — עָשָׁן הַכִּבְשָׁן when it is in construct state."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "The manuscript appears to discuss the vocalization distinguishing the verb עִשַּׁן ('he made smoke') from the noun עָשָׁן ('smoke'), using biblical examples of min + noun in construct; the text as transmitted is somewhat compressed and the clause structure has been rendered hyper-literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:42:11.030144845Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}